installation :conowingo_engagement_counter do
  name "Conowingo Engagement Counter"
  aka "the sluice-yard counter"
  tags :trade, :identity, :water, :energy

  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    A plank office at the head of the sluice yard below the Conowingo dam on the lower Susquehanna, open from the first low water in July until the ice. #{ref :market, "Market"}'s works account engages day crews there for trash-rack clearing, sediment barges and the intake gate galleries, and the office is where the corridor's #{ref :the_engagement_allotment, "allotment notes"} are filed.

    Between three and four hundred people are on its books in a season. Roughly a third of them appear in no other Market record, and the counter is built for that: it engages a crew rather than a person. A gang boss presents nine bodies, one crew name and one rate; the counter records nine, prints one contract, and the completion record afterwards names the crew and the work. Acceptance evidence out of the sluice yard covers what was done and never who did it.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :structure, heading: "The Board, The Window And The Shelf"
    The day board is chalk, and Market moves it while the yard is working. A crew that comes off at noon is paid the rate that stood in the morning, which is the one figure in the yard nobody disputes because the board's photographs go into the file with the contract.

    The note window is a separate hatch with its own clerk and its own stamp, and it will not take a note once a crew's contract has been printed. Nothing about a note can be corrected afterwards; a wrong relationship is answered by voiding the engagement and re-cutting the contract, which costs the crew the morning.

    The surrendered-note file stands behind the window: about eleven hundred notes on a one-year shelf, of which something under a fifth name the same forty-odd allottees, season after season. Anybody may ask the clerk whether a named person has been an allottee at this counter, and the clerk answers, because a filed note is part of a contract and contracts in Market territory are open.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :operations, heading: "What The Counter Cannot Do"
    It holds no reserve. The works account funds the price with Market, the counter prints the completion record, and everything after that is a matter for #{ref :claim_escrow, "escrow"} and, when a payee cannot be joined to a record, for the #{ref :race_street_claim_house, "claim house at Race Street"} eighty kilometres up the corridor.

    It also cannot refuse a crew. A gang boss who presents nine bodies and a crew name is engaged if the work is on the board, whatever the counter can establish about the nine. This sends the claim house the exact identity dispute the counter was built to avoid. Market keeps the split because naming every worker at engagement would exclude the unregistered crews that clear the dam.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — Sediment is the yard's standing work because the reservoir behind the dam filled with it: a lower Susquehanna hydro pool that had lost most of its trapping capacity by the middle of the last century. That decided the season — the counter opens at low water in July, when the barges can work the pool — and it decided why unskilled day crews are engaged in numbers rather than by name."
end
